ROOT=Path.cwd(); ART=ROOT/"articles"; BACK=ROOT/"backup-bloco-04-artigos"; BACK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
DATA=[{'filename': 'como-crear-un-jardin-vertical-en-casa.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Vertical Garden at Home and Make Better Use of Your Walls', 'category': 'GARDENING', 'meta': 'A complete guide to creating a vertical garden at home: location, structure, plants, watering, drainage, design, and maintenance to use wall space safely.', 'intro': 'A complete guide to creating a vertical garden at home: location, structure, plants, watering, drainage, design, and maintenance to use wall space safely.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Introduction</li><li>Choose the Right Location for the Vertical Garden</li><li>Select a Safe, Well-Proportioned Structure</li>\n<li>Choose Plants That Are Compatible With One Another</li><li>Plan Watering, Drainage, and Wall Protection</li>\n<li>Design an Attractive Composition Without Overcrowding</li><li>Create a Simple Maintenance Routine</li>\n<li>Common Mistakes When Creating a Vertical Garden</li><li>Adapt the Project to Everyday Life</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A vertical garden is a practical way to introduce plants into the home while making better use of walls. The difference between an attractive idea and a system that continues to work for years usually comes down to planning. Before buying supports, containers, or plants, observe the real conditions, take measurements, and think about the routines of the people who use the home.</p>\n<p>The goal is not to copy an inspirational photograph exactly. A successful vertical garden should be adapted to the available space, light, maintenance time, and budget. When these factors guide the project, it is possible to create an appealing feature without introducing solutions that later become difficult to water, clean, repair, or maintain.</p>\n<h2>Choose the Right Location for the Vertical Garden</h2>\n<p>Before buying supports or plants, observe the wall for several days. The amount of light, window orientation, ventilation, and proximity to a water source will influence almost every later decision. A bright wall does not necessarily receive direct sunlight, and that distinction determines which species are likely to remain healthy. Also check for drafts, heaters, air-conditioning outlets, or appliances that may dry the surrounding air. The best position provides reasonably stable conditions without interfering with circulation or the everyday use of the room.</p>\n<p>The wall should be dry, stable, and capable of supporting the weight of the complete system. Consider the structure, containers, growing medium, mature plants, and the extra weight of water after irrigation. In a rented home or on a delicate wall, a freestanding structure or vertical shelving unit may be more appropriate than a fixed installation. Leaving a small gap between containers and the wall improves ventilation and makes leaks or persistent moisture easier to detect.</p>\n<p>Accessibility also matters. A very tall vertical garden may look impressive, but it becomes inconvenient if every watering session requires a ladder. Keep plants that need regular observation, pruning, or harvesting at a comfortable height. Upper areas are better reserved for plants that require less frequent attention. Designing for access from the beginning makes routine care much easier.</p>\n<h2>Select a Safe, Well-Proportioned Structure</h2>\n<p>Vertical gardens can use modular systems, pocket panels, planters attached to a trellis, shelving, or structures made from wood or metal. There is no universally best system. The right choice depends on wall size, budget, plant weight, and whether drilling is possible. For a first project, a small modular system can be useful because it allows you to learn how the space behaves before covering an entire wall. Individual removable containers also make it easier to isolate a struggling plant or change the arrangement.</p>\n<p>Fasteners and anchors must match both the wall material and the expected load. A fixing designed for solid masonry may not be suitable for a lightweight partition. If there is any uncertainty about structural capacity, reduce the scale of the project or seek qualified help. This is especially important when the installation is above a sofa, table, bed, or circulation area. Decorative impact should never take priority over a stable installation.</p>\n<p>The structure should also allow access for cleaning and inspection. Systems that are completely closed can hide moisture, fallen leaves, drainage problems, or deterioration. A design that can be partially dismantled is easier to maintain. If wood is used, choose material appropriate for the environment and protect it from moisture as needed. For metal, corrosion-resistant finishes can extend useful life.</p>\n<h2>Choose Plants That Are Compatible With One Another</h2>\n<p>Plant selection should begin with the actual light available at the chosen wall. Many indoor plants can grow in bright indirect light, while a sunny balcony or exterior wall requires species able to tolerate stronger exposure. Grouping plants with similar light and watering requirements simplifies care and reduces the risk of keeping one species too wet while another dries out. Before purchasing, research mature size, growth habit, root space, and moisture requirements.</p>\n<p>Different growth forms can create a balanced composition. Trailing plants soften edges and partly conceal the support, compact plants add volume, and more upright species can create focal points. It is not necessary to fill every opening on the first day. Leaving some space gives plants room to mature, improves airflow, and prevents the installation from becoming overcrowded after a few months.</p>\n<p>If the vertical garden is in a kitchen, balcony, or an area used by children or pets, plant selection should reflect those conditions as well. Culinary herbs can be useful near the kitchen when sufficient light is available. In homes with pets or young children, check the suitability of each species and position any potentially problematic plant appropriately.</p>\n<h2>Plan Watering, Drainage, and Wall Protection</h2>\n<p>Water management is one of the most important parts of a vertical garden. Every container needs a clear method for controlling or draining excess water. In a small installation, manual watering can be useful because it helps you learn how quickly each plant and container dries. Larger installations may benefit from carefully regulated drip irrigation, but automatic watering still requires regular inspection of emitters, connections, trays, and drainage.</p>\n<p>Do not assume that every pot needs water on the same day. Height, container size, plant species, temperature, airflow, and light can all change the rate at which the growing medium dries. Check moisture before watering rather than following a rigid calendar. Symptoms of excessive and insufficient water can sometimes look similar, so consider the condition of the growing medium, leaves, and roots together.</p>\n<p>Protect the wall with appropriate trays, waterproof barriers, or spacing that prevents prolonged contact with moisture. After installing the system, perform several careful watering tests. Look for drips, runoff, damp areas, or water reaching furniture and flooring. Correcting a small leak at the beginning is much easier than repairing damaged paint, plaster, wood, or furnishings later.</p>\n<h2>Design an Attractive Composition Without Overcrowding</h2>\n<p>A vertical garden often looks more intentional when some visual elements are repeated. Repeating selected species, container colors, or leaf textures creates continuity and prevents the installation from looking like an accidental collection. You might choose one dominant plant and combine it with two or three complementary varieties, or work mainly with shades of green and use leaf shape as the primary contrast.</p>\n<p>Scale should relate to both the wall and nearby furniture. In a small room, a compact panel may have more impact than a structure covering the entire surface. Before drilling, mark the intended outline with painter's tape and view it from different positions. This simple test helps confirm height, width, and the relationship with doors, windows, furniture, and walking routes.</p>\n<p>Think about how the garden will look after several months of growth. Some plants will trail downward, others will spread sideways, and others will require occasional pruning. Designing for mature growth helps prevent one species from completely covering another. The composition can evolve over time by moving containers, replacing unsuccessful plants, and selectively pruning when necessary.</p>\n<h2>Create a Simple Maintenance Routine</h2>\n<p>A brief weekly inspection is generally more effective than infrequent major interventions. Check moisture, damaged leaves, insects, drainage trays, and the stability of supports. Remove dry plant material before it accumulates. If one area receives more light than another, rotating or exchanging suitable containers may help maintain a balanced display.</p>\n<p>Pruning can maintain shape and improve airflow. Use clean tools and avoid removing excessive foliage at one time. If a plant is growing poorly, do not automatically respond with more fertilizer. First review light, watering, growing medium, drainage, temperature, and root space. Many common plant problems begin with unsuitable environmental conditions rather than a lack of fertilizer.</p>\n<p>Over time, some growing medium may need renewal, containers may need replacement, and individual plants may need repotting or substitution. A healthy vertical garden is not a static installation. Keeping simple notes about which plants perform well in that particular location can make future decisions easier.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes When Creating a Vertical Garden</h2>\n<p>One frequent mistake is installing too many plants at the beginning. Initial enthusiasm can lead to filling every available opening, even though plants will grow and need ventilation. Another common problem is choosing species only for appearance without considering light or watering requirements. It is also easy to underestimate the total weight after watering. Leaving a margin for growth and load reduces these problems.</p>\n<p>Excess water deserves particular attention because a leak in a vertical installation can affect several containers before reaching the floor or wall. Water slowly, check collection trays, and observe how moisture moves through the system. If lower containers remain consistently wetter than upper ones, the watering arrangement may need adjustment.</p>\n<p>Avoid hiding the entire support structure behind decorative additions. Fasteners, containers, and drainage points should remain accessible enough to inspect and repair. A beautiful vertical garden still needs to be cleanable, partly removable, and serviceable. Designing for these tasks from the start can extend the life of the project.</p>\n<h2>Adapt the Project to Everyday Life</h2>\n<p>Observe the finished installation for several weeks before considering it final. Light changes with the seasons, and watering frequency may also change. Notice which pots dry first, which plants lean toward the window, and whether any area develops persistent moisture. These observations allow the arrangement to become genuinely adapted to the home rather than remaining purely decorative.</p>\n<p>Budget can be controlled by beginning with a small structure and expanding only after maintenance feels manageable. Reusing containers can be reasonable when they are clean, stable, safe, and provide appropriate drainage, but structural fasteners should not be improvised. Reliable supports, wall protection, and functional containers are usually more important than buying a large number of plants immediately.</p>\n<p>If the installation is near furniture, electrical outlets, or appliances, water management requires additional care. Keep suitable distance and prevent water from running toward electrical equipment. Combining vegetation and walls can be highly attractive, but the design must always respect the safety requirements of the home.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Can I create a vertical garden in a small home?</h3>\n<p>Yes. A compact panel, a trellis with several containers, or a narrow shelving unit can use a wall without taking up much floor space. The scale should match available light, structural capacity, and the amount of maintenance you can provide.</p>\n<h3>Is automatic irrigation required?</h3>\n<p>No. Manual watering can be simpler for a small vertical garden and allows close observation of individual plants. Automatic systems can be useful for larger installations, but they still need adjustment and inspection.</p>\n<h3>How can I prevent moisture damage to the wall?</h3>\n<p>Use containers with controlled drainage, collection trays or an appropriate protective barrier, leave ventilation behind the structure, and perform watering tests before considering the installation complete.</p>\n<h2>Practical Planning Before Installation</h2>\n<p>Before the final installation, place the containers or a paper template in the approximate positions and check the composition from several parts of the room. Confirm that the arrangement does not interfere with doors, windows, switches, furniture, or normal walking routes. Think about how you will reach every container for watering, pruning, cleaning, and eventual replacement. This practical rehearsal can reveal problems before any permanent fixing is made.</p>\n<p>It is also useful to plan for change. Individual plants may respond differently even when they appear to have similar needs, and seasonal light can alter the performance of the entire wall. A system that allows containers to be moved or removed makes it easier to respond to those changes without rebuilding the project. Flexibility is especially valuable for a first vertical garden.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Creating a vertical garden at home works best when decisions are based on the real use and conditions of the space. Measure carefully, observe light and ventilation, prioritize safe support and water management, choose compatible plants, and select solutions that can be maintained easily. There is no need to transform an entire wall at once. Start with a manageable project, observe how it performs, and expand or adjust it over time. A vertical garden becomes more successful when appearance, safety, plant health, and everyday practicality work together.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-crear-un-patio-acogedor.html', 'title': 'How to Create a Cozy and Functional Patio at Home', 'category': 'BACKYARD & PATIO', 'meta': 'Learn how to create a cozy patio with a comfortable layout, durable furniture, shade, plants, lighting, and practical details for enjoying the outdoors.', 'intro': 'Learn how to create a cozy patio with a comfortable layout, durable furniture, shade, plants, lighting, and practical details for enjoying the outdoors.', 'body': '\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Plan the Layout Before Decorating</li><li>Choose Comfortable, Weather-Resistant Furniture</li>\n<li>Create Shade and Protection</li><li>Use Plants for Freshness and Privacy</li><li>Light the Patio for Evening Use</li>\n<li>Add Details Without Creating Clutter</li><li>Keep the Patio Pleasant Throughout the Year</li>\n<li>Adapt the Project to Everyday Life</li><li>Make a Final Check Before Buying or Installing</li>\n<li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A cozy patio begins with practical planning. A comfortable layout, durable furniture, useful shade, suitable plants, thoughtful lighting, and a few well-chosen details can make an outdoor area much easier to enjoy. Before buying or moving anything, measure the available space, observe the conditions at different times of day, and think about how the household will actually use the patio.</p>\n<p>The objective is not to reproduce an inspiration photograph exactly. A patio that works for years needs to reflect its dimensions, climate, sunlight, wind, maintenance requirements, budget, and daily routines. When function is resolved first, decoration becomes easier and the finished space is more likely to remain comfortable instead of becoming crowded or difficult to maintain.</p>\n<h2>Plan the Layout Before Decorating</h2>\n<p>Start by measuring the patio and locating doors, windows, drains, steps, and the routes people use most often. Then decide on the main function: outdoor meals, conversation, rest, container gardening, or a combination of activities. In a small patio, choosing one primary purpose can prevent the area from filling with furniture that is rarely used.</p>\n<p>Keep circulation clear. People should be able to enter, sit down, and move around without constantly avoiding chairs or pots. In a larger patio, different zones can be suggested with an outdoor rug, groups of planters, or furniture orientation rather than permanent partitions. In a narrow space, positioning larger furniture near the perimeter can free the center and improve movement.</p>\n<p>Observe the patio at different hours. Notice where direct sun falls, where wind is strongest, which areas remain cooler, and where privacy is best. This information helps determine where a dining table, bench, or lounge chair will be most comfortable. A layout based only on appearance can be disappointing if it ignores the actual conditions.</p>\n<h2>Choose Comfortable, Weather-Resistant Furniture</h2>\n<p>Furniture should fit both the dimensions of the patio and its environmental exposure. Wood, metal, synthetic fibers, and other outdoor materials behave differently in sun, rain, humidity, and temperature changes. Before purchasing, review maintenance requirements and decide how much regular care you are willing to provide. An attractive piece that requires more upkeep than expected can quickly become inconvenient.</p>\n<p>Comfort also depends on scale. A table that is too large can block circulation, while undersized seating may become uncomfortable during longer gatherings. Folding, stackable, or modular furniture is particularly useful when the patio needs to change functions. These pieces can create additional seating when guests arrive and then be stored or rearranged to free space.</p>\n<p>Outdoor textiles add comfort and color, but they should be easy to clean or store. Removable cushion covers and fabrics intended for exterior use can simplify care. If the patio is uncovered, a storage box or nearby indoor space for cushions and textiles during severe weather can help keep them in better condition.</p>\n<h2>Create Shade and Protection</h2>\n<p>A patio exposed to strong sun may be uncomfortable during the hottest hours. Umbrellas, retractable awnings, pergolas, and vegetation can provide shade, but the solution should match orientation, wind exposure, available space, and local conditions. A movable umbrella offers flexibility, an awning can protect a larger area, and a pergola can become a permanent structure that helps organize the patio.</p>\n<p>Shade should not unnecessarily block natural light from nearby rooms. Before installing a permanent cover, consider how it will affect windows throughout the year. In some locations, an adjustable solution that can be opened during cooler months and extended during hot weather offers greater flexibility. Lightweight or fabric elements must also be secured appropriately for local wind conditions.</p>\n<p>Climbing plants can provide natural shade and soften a structure, but they need time to grow and require maintenance. Consider their mature weight, growth habit, and pruning needs before training them over a pergola or trellis. Combining a structural shade solution with vegetation can provide immediate comfort while plants establish gradually.</p>\n<h2>Use Plants for Freshness and Privacy</h2>\n<p>Plants soften hard surfaces and make a patio feel more alive. Choose them according to sunlight, climate, wind, and container size rather than appearance alone. Instead of purchasing many unrelated small plants, it can be more effective to combine a few structural plants with smaller varieties and seasonal flowers.</p>\n<p>Grouping pots can make watering easier and create stronger visual impact. Different container heights or plant forms add depth, but repeating a material, color, or plant type can keep the arrangement coherent. When floor area is limited, walls, railings, shelves, and appropriate vertical structures can increase planting space without blocking circulation.</p>\n<p>For privacy, large containers with suitable shrubs or climbing plants on a trellis can filter views without completely enclosing the patio. Research mature root and branch growth before choosing. A plant that looks compact at the nursery may require substantially more space after several years.</p>\n<h2>Light the Patio for Evening Use</h2>\n<p>Outdoor lighting should make movement safe and create atmosphere without making the patio excessively bright. Soft general lighting can be complemented by carefully positioned light near steps, routes, or dining areas. String lights and portable lamps may add warmth, while directional lighting can highlight selected plants or textures.</p>\n<p>Any electrical equipment should be suitable for outdoor use and for the level of water exposure at its location. Improvised cables, plugs, and connections are not an appropriate permanent solution. When permanent electrical work is required, use qualified assistance as appropriate to local requirements. Safety is more important than decorative effect.</p>\n<p>Solar lights can be useful in some areas, but performance depends on how much sunlight the panel actually receives. Testing one or two units in the intended position before purchasing many can prevent disappointment. Different light sources can then be combined according to whether the patio is being used for dinner, quiet conversation, or simple nighttime circulation.</p>\n<h2>Add Details Without Creating Clutter</h2>\n<p>Small details can give the patio personality. An outdoor rug, a few distinctive planters, coordinated textiles, or a useful tray may be enough to transform the atmosphere. Too many accessories, however, reduce usable space and increase cleaning and storage needs. Choose fewer elements with a clear purpose rather than filling every available surface.</p>\n<p>Color can be introduced through plants and textiles, which are easier to change than the main furniture. A relatively neutral base can be refreshed with small seasonal adjustments. Natural-looking materials, fibers, and wood can add warmth when they are suitable for the local outdoor conditions and maintained appropriately.</p>\n<p>Storage is part of the design as well. Watering cans, small tools, cushions, and accessories need a defined place. A bench with internal storage or a compact outdoor cabinet can help keep the patio clear. A tidy space often feels more welcoming because the functional surfaces remain available for everyday use.</p>\n<h2>Keep the Patio Pleasant Throughout the Year</h2>\n<p>A welcoming patio needs a maintenance routine proportionate to its materials and climate. Sweeping leaves, cleaning surfaces, and checking textiles before dirt accumulates can keep larger cleaning sessions from becoming necessary. The frequency will depend on vegetation, weather, and whether the patio is covered.</p>\n<p>At seasonal changes, inspect furniture screws and joints, protective finishes, corrosion, and signs of persistent moisture. Plants may also need pruning, repotting, or seasonal protection. Carrying out these checks before periods of heavy rain, heat, or cold can help prevent deterioration and keep the space ready for use.</p>\n<p>Not everything has to remain outside all year. Storing textiles, umbrellas, or accessories during periods when they are not used can extend their useful life. A flexible patio that changes with the seasons and activities is often more practical than one designed as a completely fixed composition.</p>\n<h2>Adapt the Project to Everyday Life</h2>\n<p>Before investing heavily, use the patio for several days with a temporary arrangement. Existing chairs, boxes representing planters, or tape marking the footprint of a future table can reveal whether the idea works. This trial can show where shade is necessary, which paths need to stay open, and which part of the patio feels most comfortable later in the day.</p>\n<p>Budget is usually better spent first on the elements that directly affect use: comfortable seating, stable surfaces, necessary shade, and appropriate lighting. Decoration can be added afterward. Buying many accessories before solving basic comfort often produces a patio that looks finished but does not function well in daily life.</p>\n<p>Consider who will use the area. Children, pets, older adults, and guests may benefit from stable furniture, wider routes, or surfaces selected with safety and maintenance in mind. Adapting the patio to its actual users makes it more likely to be enjoyed regularly.</p>\n<h2>Make a Final Check Before Buying or Installing</h2>\n<p>Walk mentally through an ordinary day on the patio. Check where someone will place a drink, whether chairs can move without colliding, whether people can pass when seats are occupied, and where objects that should not remain outdoors will be stored. These simple actions can reveal problems that are not obvious in a floor plan.</p>\n<p>Also observe how water behaves during rain or cleaning. Drains must remain accessible, and furniture should not prevent water from leaving the area. If you add rugs, large pots, decking, or other coverings, make sure they do not create places where water remains trapped. A patio that drains and dries effectively is generally easier to clean and maintain.</p>\n<p>Finally, leave room for change. Household needs may evolve, and plants will grow. A flexible layout makes it possible to move a table, add a seat, or remove a planter without redesigning the entire space. Adaptability is one of the most useful qualities for keeping a patio comfortable over time.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What should I do first to improve a patio?</h3>\n<p>Measure the area, observe sun and wind, and decide how the patio will primarily be used. With that information, plan the layout before buying furniture or decorations.</p>\n<h3>How can I make a small patio feel cozy?</h3>\n<p>Prioritize one main function, use furniture that fits the scale, take advantage of vertical planting where appropriate, and add soft lighting and a few textiles without blocking circulation.</p>\n<h3>What type of furniture is best for a patio?</h3>\n<p>Choose furniture designed for outdoor use, correctly sized for the available space, and compatible with the amount of maintenance you are willing to provide. The ideal material depends on climate and exposure.</p>\n<h2>Balance Comfort, Appearance, and Maintenance</h2>\n<p>A patio becomes easier to enjoy when attractive choices also support practical routines. Leave useful surfaces free, keep frequently used items within reach, and avoid placing delicate accessories where they must constantly be moved because of sun, rain, or circulation. The best arrangement is one that remains convenient after the initial decorating phase.</p>\n<p>Review the patio after several weeks of real use. If chairs are always moved to another position, a planter blocks the preferred route, or a shaded corner is used more than the original seating area, treat that behavior as useful information. Adjusting the layout to actual habits can improve the space more effectively than adding more decoration.</p><h2>Balance Comfort, Appearance, and Maintenance</h2>\n<p>A patio becomes easier to enjoy when attractive choices also support practical routines. Leave useful surfaces free, keep frequently used items within reach, and avoid placing delicate accessories where they must constantly be moved because of sun, rain, or circulation. The best arrangement is one that remains convenient after the initial decorating phase.</p>\n<p>Review the patio after several weeks of real use. If chairs are always moved to another position, a planter blocks the preferred route, or a shaded corner is used more than the original seating area, treat that behavior as useful information. Adjusting the layout to actual habits can improve the space more effectively than adding more decoration.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Creating a cozy and functional patio works best when the design starts with real use. Measure the space, observe environmental conditions, prioritize circulation and comfort, and choose solutions that can be maintained without unnecessary effort. There is no need to transform everything at once. Begin with changes that solve clear needs, test how they perform, and add decorative details afterward. A patio feels most welcoming when appearance supports comfort, order, flexibility, and everyday use.</p>\n'}]
def wc(h): return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b",re.sub(r"<[^>]+>"," ",h)))
